DAILY BRIEF =C2=B7 SEMICONDUCTORS=20
Lam Research hit a 40% operating margin for the first time and still fell 7% =

Revenue rose 54% to a record $8.22 billion on memory-chip demand. Guidance=20
only matched forecasts, supply constraints limited the outlook, and China=E2=
=80=99s=20
share of sales is expected to fall.=20
Lam Research reported record revenue of $8.22 billion for its September=20
quarter on Wednesday, up 54% from a year earlier, and adjusted earnings of=20
$2.20 a share, slightly above consensus, according to 24/7 Wall St. Its=20
operating margin =E2=80=9Cexceeded 40% for the first time in our history,=E2=
=80=9D Lam=E2=80=99s chief=20
financial officer, Doug Bettinger, said, and its adjusted gross margin of 52.=
4%=20
was a record. For the December quarter, Lam forecast revenue of $8.6 billion,=
=20
plus or minus $400 million, against a consensus of about $8.54 billion, and=20
earnings of $2.33 a share, plus or minus 15 cents. Bettinger said guidance=20
=E2=80=9Cwould have been a bit higher if not for some near-term supply chain =

constraints.=E2=80=9D The shares, up about 81% over the past year, fell 7.4% =
after=20
hours to $241.57. Chief Executive Tim Archer said the industry=E2=80=99s spen=
ding on=20
wafer fabrication equipment would be in the mid-$150 billion range in 2026 an=
d=20
more than $200 billion in 2027, led by memory chips.=20
The latest dashboard=20

$8.22B Record quarterly revenue, up 54% 40.0% Operating margin, a company fir=
st
=E2=88=927.4% Shares after hours >$200B Lam=E2=80=99s preliminary 2027 chip-e=
quipment market=20
outlook=20
Why a record quarter sold off=20
Expectations were high. Lam=E2=80=99s stock had risen about 81% in a year, an=
d=20
investors had likely expected a forecast well above consensus. Instead, the=20
midpoint of guidance roughly matched estimates, and the low ends of the reven=
ue=20
and earnings ranges fell below them, 24/7 Wall St. noted. China is a second=20
factor. China accounted for 35% of Lam=E2=80=99s revenue in the quarter, up f=
rom 26% in=20
June, and Bettinger said he expects China=E2=80=99s share to be =E2=80=9Cmean=
ingfully down=E2=80=9D in=20
December. Sales to China have been volatile because of U.S. export=20
restrictions, and Chinese chipmakers such as CXMT are expanding fast. The=20
selloff also reflects the broader nervousness in chip stocks. The semiconduct=
or=20
index fell more than 15% over eight sessions earlier this month before=20
rebounding, as investors questioned AI spending and financing.=20
The demand signal is still strong=20
Lam makes etch and deposition equipment used heavily in memory chip=20
production, including the high-bandwidth memory used alongside AI processors.=
=20
Archer=E2=80=99s forecast that equipment spending will exceed $200 billion in=
 2027, up=20
from about $150 billion this year, is one of the most bullish industry outloo=
ks=20
yet, and it is led by DRAM memory. The limit is physical capacity. Archer=20
warned that limited clean room space and component shortages will cap how muc=
h=20
of 2027 demand can be met, which supports pricing but limits volume growth. F=
or=20
investors in AI, Lam=E2=80=99s outlook suggests chipmakers are still planning=
 to expand=20
capacity aggressively next year. That is a different message from the credit =

market worries that hit CoreWeave and other AI infrastructure companies last =

week.=20
What would change this reading=20
Watch memory prices, Lam=E2=80=99s China sales in the December quarter and sp=
ending=20
plans from memory makers such as SK Hynix, Samsung and Micron. Confirmation o=
f=20
a $200 billion-plus equipment market in 2027 would support the stock; cuts to=
=20
memory spending would undercut it. The practical monitoring rule is to separa=
te=20
three stages. First comes the signal reported now. Second comes confirmation =

from a different source or design. Third comes evidence that the signal chang=
es=20
outcomes, behavior or resource allocation in the expected direction. Keeping =

those stages distinct prevents an interesting association, estimate or policy=
=20
statement from being promoted too quickly into a universal rule. It also make=
s=20
the editorial useful after the next headline arrives, because readers know=20
exactly what evidence should update the view.=20

DIAGNOSTIC Lam=E2=80=99s business is at record strength, driven by memory dem=
and, but=20
the stock had priced in more. Supply limits and a China pullback tempered the=
=20
outlook.=20
A five-part reading framework=20
1 Results
Revenue $8.22B, +54%; adjusted EPS $2.20; operating margin 40%.=20
2 Guidance
$8.6B =C2=B1 $400M revenue; EPS $2.33 =C2=B1 $0.15; near consensus.=20
3 China
35% of revenue, expected to fall =E2=80=9Cmeaningfully=E2=80=9D in December. =

4 Industry outlook
Equipment spending mid-$150B in 2026, above $200B in 2027.=20
5 Constraints
Clean room space and component shortages.=20

DAILY BRIEF TAKEAWAY Lam delivered its best quarter ever, and investors sold =

because it was not better. Its outlook still points to a large expansion in=20
chipmaking capacity next year.=20
Source: 24/7 Wall St., Oct. 22, 2026, citing Lam Research results and=20
Investing.com consensus.
